WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.576
Industrial network traffic is a foundational telemetry source.

00:00:03.576 --> 00:00:07.486
It shows which devices communicate, the protocols they use,

00:00:07.486 --> 00:00:10.093
the direction and timing of connections,

00:00:10.093 --> 00:00:14.872
and in some cases the industrial operations represented by those messages.

00:00:14.872 --> 00:00:20.085
Traffic at trust boundaries is especially useful for understanding movement between enterprise,

00:00:20.085 --> 00:00:21.823
site, and control functions.

00:00:21.823 --> 00:00:26.168
Controllers and PLC-related communications can reveal logic transfers, mode changes,

00:00:26.168 --> 00:00:27.037
programming sessions,

00:00:27.037 --> 00:00:32.251
and unusual peers when the protocol and collection point provide that visibility.

00:00:32.251 --> 00:00:37.464
HMIs and SCADA applications may record operator logons, alarms, commands, application faults,

00:00:37.464 --> 00:00:38.768
and communication loss.

00:00:38.768 --> 00:00:43.112
Engineering workstations offer high-value evidence because they contain privileged tools.

00:00:43.112 --> 00:00:47.891
Endpoint, application, and authentication logs can show users, processes, removable media,

00:00:47.891 --> 00:00:50.933
project changes, and connections to control devices.

00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:54.843
Collection must be tested to avoid affecting supported applications.

00:00:54.843 --> 00:00:57.884
Historians provide process trends and operating context.

00:00:57.884 --> 00:01:04.401
They can help investigators compare a cyber observation with changes in equipment state or production.

00:01:04.401 --> 00:01:09.180
Historian access logs and replication paths may also reveal cross-boundary activity.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.959
Remote-access gateways, VPNs, jump hosts, and vendor platforms should record identity,

00:01:13.959 --> 00:01:17.869
session times, destination, approval, and sensitive actions where feasible.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:22.649
Firewalls and network devices provide permitted and denied connections, configuration changes,

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:24.821
link status, and boundary events.

00:01:24.821 --> 00:01:28.297
Identity systems contribute authentication, privilege, and account-lifecycle evidence.

00:01:28.297 --> 00:01:31.338
Telemetry quality matters more than raw volume.

00:01:31.338 --> 00:01:34.814
Time synchronization, asset identity, retention, integrity, access control,

00:01:34.814 --> 00:01:38.289
and clear ownership determine whether records support investigation.

00:01:38.289 --> 00:01:43.069
Build a source map that explains what each system can report,

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:46.979
what it cannot see, how long data is retained,

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:50.020
and who responds when the source fails.
